Why do we buy homes? 
/// It doesn't take a masters degree in behavioral economics to understand why we value homes. They give us shelter, saftey, and a place to eat at the basic level. Instead, I want to engage your mind to think deeper about why we buy a house instead of rent one. Let's say you work your ass off for 10-20 years to save up enough money to buy a home with 100% cash. You come to an agreement with the seller where you willingly exchange your money for the property and all that comes with it. What else is there that we value besides those basic needs? Maybe the location is conviently near your work, friends, and family. Why not just rent the same house for a cheaper amount with more flexiblity? Well, you are granted property rights that are not protected when you rent a property from someone. If you decide to raise kids, then you want stablity in the home that they live in and want to avoid looking for a new lease to sign every 12 or 24 months. Okay, but how realistic is it for the average person to buy a house with outright cash? Not at all, so we have banks that we go into contract with instead of a property owner. The bank gives anyone the option to take out a mortgage to buy a house which strectes the cost over 15 or 30 years at exchange for intrest payments each month. As long as you make those agreed upon payments every month the house will be yours outright in however long. Now the buyer of a home has much more flexiblity regarding the leverage they have on their asset compared to the renter who is not builidng any real estate of sorts. We've established the generic reasons why it is valuable to buy a house but if you're going to buy a house you must first understand the economic levers that are being pulled which influences the price of homes as well as the price of the loan you might take out. 
---
How intrest rates work? 
/// The FED, a private institution governed by a group of 7 board members, has direct control over the supply of money or liquidity in the markets based on what value that set rates at. The Fed has the option to pull on three major levers: the federal funds rate, the discount rate, and federal open market operations. Think of the federal funds rate as the goal they are setting and the discount rate and the open market operations are used to reach their target. 
